workedExamples.solution_text:
Find the intersections.
$\begin{align*}x^{2}&=x+12\\x^{2}-x-12&=0\\(x-4)(x+3)&=0\\x&=-3,\ 4\end{align*}$
At $x=0$, the line has height $12$ and the parabola has height $0$, so the line is upper.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.65cm,y=0.19cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{regionfill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[regionfill] plot[domain=-3:4,samples=2] (\x,{\x+12}) -- plot[domain=4:-3,samples=65] (\x,{\x*\x}) -- cycle;
\draw[black,->] (-4,0)--(5,0);
\draw[black,->] (0,-2)--(0,22);
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-3.5:4.5,samples=65] (\x,{\x*\x});
\draw[black,thick] (-3.7,8.3)--(4.7,16.7);
\path (5,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,22) node[above] {$y$} (-2,17) node {$y=x+12$} (3,5) node {$y=x^{2}$};
\draw[black,dashed] (-3,0)--(-3,9) (4,0)--(4,16);
\path (-3,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$-3$} (4,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$4$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
The light blue region lies between the curves.
$\begin{align*}A&=\int_{-3}^{4}(x+12-x^{2})\,dx\\&=\left[\frac{x^{2}}{2}+12x-\frac{x^{3}}{3}\right]_{-3}^{4}\\&=\frac{104}{3}-\left(-\frac{45}{2}\right)\\&=\frac{343}{6}\ \text{units}^{2}\end{align*}$

Find where the curves meet.
$\begin{align*}x^{2}&=(x-8)^{2}\\x^{2}&=x^{2}-16x+64\\16x&=64\\x&=4\end{align*}$
The curves meet the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=8$. The required region lies under $y=x^{2}$ on $[0,4]$, then under $y=(x-8)^{2}$ on $[4,8]$.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.6cm,y=0.19cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{regionfill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[regionfill] (0,0)--plot[domain=0:4,samples=45] (\x,{\x*\x})--plot[domain=4:8,samples=45] (\x,{(\x-8)*(\x-8)})--cycle;
\draw[black,->] (-1,0)--(9,0);
\draw[black,->] (0,-2)--(0,23);
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-0.5:4.6,samples=50] (\x,{\x*\x});
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=3.4:8.5,samples=50] (\x,{(\x-8)*(\x-8)});
\draw[black,dashed] (4,0)--(4,16);
\path (9,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,23) node[above] {$y$} (1.7,14) node {$y=x^{2}$} (7,17) node {$y=(x-8)^{2}$};
\path (4,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$4$} (8,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$8$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
Split at $x=4$ and add the two areas above the axis.
$\begin{align*}A_{1}&=\int_{0}^{4}x^{2}\,dx\\&=\left[\frac{x^{3}}{3}\right]_{0}^{4}\\&=\frac{64}{3}\end{align*}$
$\begin{align*}A_{2}&=\int_{4}^{8}(x-8)^{2}\,dx\\&=\left[\frac{(x-8)^{3}}{3}\right]_{4}^{8}\\&=0-\left(-\frac{64}{3}\right)\\&=\frac{64}{3}\end{align*}$
$\begin{align*}A&=A_{1}+A_{2}\\&=\frac{128}{3}\ \text{units}^{2}\end{align*}$

Find the intersections.
$\begin{align*}x^{2}&=4(x-9)^{2}\\3x^{2}-72x+324&=0\\3(x-6)(x-18)&=0\\x&=6,\ 18\end{align*}$
The curves meet the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=9$. The region bounded by both curves and the axis uses the crossing at $x=6$; the crossing at $x=18$ belongs to a different region.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.5cm,y=0.1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{regionfill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[regionfill] (0,0)--plot[domain=0:6,samples=50] (\x,{\x*\x})--plot[domain=6:9,samples=50] (\x,{4*(\x-9)*(\x-9)})--cycle;
\draw[black,->] (-1,0)--(10,0);
\draw[black,->] (0,-4)--(0,52);
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-0.5:7,samples=55] (\x,{\x*\x});
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=5.5:9.6,samples=55] (\x,{4*(\x-9)*(\x-9)});
\draw[black,dashed] (6,0)--(6,36);
\path (10,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,52) node[above] {$y$} (2.6,28) node {$y=x^{2}$} (10,46) node[right] {$y=4(x-9)^{2}$};
\path (6,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$6$} (9,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$9$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
The required region lies under $y=x^{2}$ on $[0,6]$, then under $y=4(x-9)^{2}$ on $[6,9]$. The lower boundary is the $x$-axis.
$\begin{align*}A_{1}&=\int_{0}^{6}x^{2}\,dx\\&=\left[\frac{x^{3}}{3}\right]_{0}^{6}\\&=72\end{align*}$
$\begin{align*}A_{2}&=\int_{6}^{9}4(x-9)^{2}\,dx\\&=\left[\frac{4(x-9)^{3}}{3}\right]_{6}^{9}\\&=0-(-36)\\&=36\end{align*}$
$\begin{align*}A&=A_{1}+A_{2}\\&=108\ \text{units}^{2}\end{align*}$

